cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it together with the two sources of the proof it reads (see references)."
title: "Why the Lean proof of the Riemann Hypothesis works: Stepanov's balance in rings and arcs, and where the exponent 1/2 is set"
abstract: >-
  A structural reading of the Lean 4 proof of the Riemann Hypothesis and of GRH for Dirichlet
  L-functions by Levent Alpoge (with Claude), in the compact rewriting grh-compact by Luke Sernau
  and colleagues at Anthropic (with Claude). The proof is read as Stepanov's balance over the
  integers: a chain of m theta lifts wins multiplicity m, the level prime N is charged twice, and
  the square law m^2 <~ x sets the exponent 1/2. The Lean files under Structural/ build on the
  unchanged grh-compact and state these steps as theorems. As an application, Lean deductions
  from Mathlib's RiemannHypothesis of psi(x) = x + O(x^(1/2+delta)) and of a prime in
  (x, x + x^(1/2+epsilon)] for large x (von Koch 1901); the conditional versions are registered
  on Palomar as PALOMAR-2026-11-26-000056. The proof of the Riemann Hypothesis is not a result of
  this work.
